 to bring up a couple terms  that you may or may not be familiar with  one of which is viable but  non-culturable  organisms these organisms are viable but  because of environmental stress placed  upon them  they will not be recovered in culture  but they're still virulent  and they're capable of causing disease  usually the end product or the  consequence of this  is chronic type of infections or  symptoms  with seemingly no known cause another  group are what we call  persisters they are tolerant to  antibiotics  but refractory to treatment so as a  result of that  culture will miss those and as a result  of that  specimens also  and i think everyone will agree with  this when you get a specimen in  from surgery it's pan culture  mycomycology  mycobacteriology bacteriology  and possibly a molecular assay  that requires a lot of media and  and labor and as a result of that it's  costly  the cost of ngs is coming down  significantly and the limited  comparisons i've done with costs  yes ngs is a little more expensive but  not significantly more expensive  than the pan culturing that is common  with many of these specimens that come  in  so it's a reasonable cost and once again  the results  have been available and continue to be  available  in less than 96 hours some organisms  require up to 96 hours to grow i.e  cardio bacterium is one of those we hold  ours for 11 days  before we call them negative next gen  will detect them in less than 96 hours  and that level one they may be detected  in a quantitative  pcr and i've given you a reference there  at the bottom that gives you a nice  overview  of ngs  so what has been our experience in  adding ngs to the test menu  well it started with an orthopedic  surgeon coming to me and he says have  you thought about  providing ngs2 to our service  and obviously the answer was no and i  said  i would look into it and i met with the  representative  from microgen dx and we discussed it  learned more about it and i went to  another orthopedic surgeon and i need to  clarify  we support because we're a core  laboratory  two orthopedic groups  uh one deals with arthroplasty  and revisions the other deals with  trauma  i.e fractures so meeting with the  orthopedic surgeon that's associated  with trauma  and our institution where the core  laboratory is located is primarily a  trauma center  and we discussed it and he suggested why  don't we do a pilot study  and determine get some experience  and determine if we should pursue this  even further  and we agreed to do that so we  contacted the company and they agreed to  support  a pilot study of approximately 20  patients  at no charge and then they provided us  we had to learn and we're still on a  learning curve because we've been doing  this now for  just a little over two years and  microgen diagnostic  provided us with the collection  guidelines  which include four swabs joint fluid  or pus and tissue in addition to the  specimen  and shipping containers and the goal was  to compare it with  conventional culture and to determine  the  level of concordance between culture and  ngs  you know we also have to consider  administrative logistics  if we go with this what's going to be  the charge to the laboratory  we have to establish a contract who is  responsible for the shipping containers  to use fedex  how are we going to get the reports who  gets the reports  and then one of my key factors was  i want to see their their validation  data  i want to know if they're licensed by  clia are they cap accredited how are  they doing in their proficiency testing  i won't go into the charges to the  laboratory because that's going to be a  result  or be influenced by your volume and etc  we did establish a contract  they provide the fedex shipping  costs in addition to the collection  containers and so forth  and i would recommend that you go on  youtube  video and pull up microgen dx they have  a very good and recent  specimen collection video and 