y determine  their clinical relevance  because in many of these cases they fail  to get histological examinations  and send tissue for that purpose and we  we try to encourage and influence the  clinicians if you're going to order  culture on tissue  please order histopathology because if  you can demonstrate the organism  in the tissue there's a good chance that  that organism is clinically relevant  so of the four here i could not  determine  nor could the infectious disease people  determine if these other organisms were  contaminants  or just collected or obtained during  collection  so what is the current status of the  future applications of next generation  sequencing  well one of the problems we have  encountered is the fact  that there's been a lot of inconsistency  with collecting the recommended  number of specimens that is a part of  this ngs program  the specimens that should be collected  are four swabs  tissue and fluid if available  and that's been a problem with the  specimens at least that we received  and the lack of consistency in getting  all those specimens at one time  i know from talking with our  counterparts of microgen dx  the preferred specimens for their work  are the four swabs uh with the tissue  and the fluid can be retained by the  laboratory  uh for for culture but once again that's  something you have to work out  with your providers regarding uh  do we culture them all or do we just  send four swabs and  those are some of the details we're  working out with our respective  providers  the logistics related to responsible  party for shipment of specimens  because we are a core laboratory that  one orthopedic group  is in another facility we've resolved  that  satellite laboratory if you will is  responsible for sending those specimens  for next gen sequencing so to this point  we're satisfied with the service which  includes turnaround time  their responsiveness and the reasonable  cost that they charge per specimen  and the physicians are seem to be very  satisfied  and i like to interact with physicians i  believe microbiologists or directors  need to get out of their silos and  interact with the medical  staff let them know who we are what we  do that also includes the nurses  it's very valuable for identifying  sterile or unidentifiable molds  and the sooner you get the answer the  better it is  hopefully for the patient if it's a  contaminant fine but at least we know  what it is  we are sending bals  that have been in patients with no  detectable infectious agent  patient is still very ill so we'll take  the bal  we'll send it the next gen and we  usually are very successful  in coming up with a pathogen this even  applies to positive or  blood cultures that are negative when  you know that through the gram stain  there's something there  uh the morphology and we've sent a  couple blood cultures down  for next-gen sequencing and have come  back positive  for the organism that we could not  recover in culture  so for me interacting with orthopedic  surgeons has been a very uh  fun activity we get rare and new  organisms  clinical relevance is the big question  and  at least in our learning curve i get  that question  quite a bit from the orthopedic surgeons  and my comment is because i don't know  i'm not a practicing physician i say  what does your patient look like  i think with the addition of the  resistance  phase for the susceptibility or the  predictability of  response to those antibiotics that helps  them in terms of  clinical relevance but they make the  final clinical decisions  so but i think the clinical  microbiologist without a doubt has to be  involved  from day one not only from the  implementation  considering both the technical as well  as administrative aspect  but be available to discuss through  uh result interpretation uh  what these organisms are what they've  been associated with  and when we get a rare or unusual  organism we've never heard of  uh we will do a literature search and  and and pick out a couple recent papers  and on the report we will put the the  journal  title the volume the year we  and we don't send the paper but they  have the reference that they want to  read about it  and then of course fungal id uh has  already proven itself  uh we are getting ready to uh  use next gen for cystic fibrosis  patients which is  a biofilm in itself and several  organisms in there  that we know we're not culturing but  they're there so  that has yet to start but it soon will  be online after the first  year and then some of the eent  physicians around town are beginning to  call and we have  uh started with one eent group and as a  result of that  it's too early to tell but very  interested in that  so by way of summary for microbiology  ngs is in its infancy as far as the  laboratory is concerned  uh so we outsource it right now  hopefully someday we can bring in  nextgen into our laboratory uh  and work with our hematopathologists uh  once again we're in an era of rapid  diagnostics  traditional culture is still the gold  standard but you know as well as i do  that days uh are are not uncommon in  getting positive results  but rapid diagnostics is the error that  we're in and will continue to be  once again we're dealing with a  situation at least in the orthopedic  world  poly microbial infections we have to  deal with that  and i mentioned the viable but  non-conservable organisms  the optimal utilization of ngs that's  been a question asked of mike  from my colleagues is there an optimal  way to use  ngs rather than do it on every situation  i think it depends upon  the clinicians you're working with but i  would definitely  recommend it if it's been culture  negative do ngs  and whether or not you pair with culture  i think is a question that you have to  work out with your colleagues  outcome studies are critically needed i  know dr parvisi is doing some of those  and what we really need are outcome  studies to show 